WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.500 --> 00:00:05.895
Welcome to Horizon installer
training, Part 7. The last part.

2
00:00:06.345 --> 00:00:08.499
Old systems and retrofits.

3
00:00:08.949 --> 00:00:12.197
By the end, you will know which
system you are standing under,

4
00:00:12.197 --> 00:00:15.433
when to stop and call Ben,
and what to tell the customer.

5
00:00:15.883 --> 00:00:18.989
You will also learn the
three ways we fix an old job.

6
00:00:20.339 --> 00:00:24.235
On a service call, find out
which system it is, first.

7
00:00:24.685 --> 00:00:28.371
There are three. Mark
1, Mark 2, and Mark 3.

8
00:00:28.821 --> 00:00:32.229
Do not change a Mark 1
or a Mark 2 in the field.

9
00:00:32.679 --> 00:00:34.891
They get booked for a proper retrofit.

10
00:00:36.241 --> 00:00:39.794
Mark 1 is the old boxes,
from our first jobs.

11
00:00:39.794 --> 00:00:42.807
It runs a 350 watt, 12 volt supply.

12
00:00:43.257 --> 00:00:47.908
It has one wire per injection. That part
is right. But there are no breakers.

13
00:00:48.358 --> 00:00:51.349
Do not touch it. Write down
the address, and tell Ben.

14
00:00:51.349 --> 00:00:53.925
It needs a new box, controller and supply.

15
00:00:54.375 --> 00:00:58.804
Mark 2 is our box with the old
board, on a 24 volt supply.

16
00:00:59.254 --> 00:01:02.408
It often has one 14/2
feeding several injections,

17
00:01:02.408 --> 00:01:05.553
and only two 5 amp
breakers in the whole box.

18
00:01:06.003 --> 00:01:09.097
Do not touch it. It is
first in line for retrofit.

19
00:01:09.097 --> 00:01:12.256
It needs new wire or
drivers, not just a board.

20
00:01:12.706 --> 00:01:19.132
Mark 3 is our box with the new board,
and six breakers. 600 watts, at 24 volts.

21
00:01:19.582 --> 00:01:22.662
One feeder per circuit,
a 4 amp breaker each,

22
00:01:22.662 --> 00:01:25.649
and the positive cut at every injection.

23
00:01:26.099 --> 00:01:28.606
That is the current
standard. Normal service.

24
00:01:28.606 --> 00:01:30.807
Stay inside the limits from Part 2.

25
00:01:32.157 --> 00:01:35.042
Stop, and call Ben, if
you see any of these.

26
00:01:35.492 --> 00:01:39.852
Lights wired straight into the
power supply. No breaker, no fuse.

27
00:01:40.302 --> 00:01:43.246
One wire feeding two or
more injection points.

28
00:01:43.246 --> 00:01:46.183
Or more than 70 lights between injections.

29
00:01:46.633 --> 00:01:48.218
Any 12 volt system.

30
00:01:48.668 --> 00:01:51.647
A breaker that trips when
the customer picks white.

31
00:01:52.097 --> 00:01:56.956
Wire going through a wall with
no ETL, CSA or UL number on it.

32
00:01:57.406 --> 00:02:01.534
Wire that looks brown, stiff
or melted, or smells burnt.

33
00:02:01.984 --> 00:02:05.612
And solid copper wire. Everything
we run must be stranded.

34
00:02:06.962 --> 00:02:09.662
And there are three things
you never do on an old system.

35
00:02:10.112 --> 00:02:12.440
Never add lights to a Mark 1 or a Mark 2.

36
00:02:12.440 --> 00:02:16.608
Those circuits are already full.
More lights is what starts the fire.

37
00:02:17.058 --> 00:02:19.816
Never fit a bigger breaker
to stop it tripping.

38
00:02:20.266 --> 00:02:23.232
And never leave an old run
live without metering it.

39
00:02:24.582 --> 00:02:27.932
The customer may ask what is going
on. Here is what you tell them.

40
00:02:28.382 --> 00:02:31.297
We moved to a new power
system. It is more reliable,

41
00:02:31.297 --> 00:02:33.509
and it protects the circuit better.

42
00:02:33.959 --> 00:02:35.927
You are on our list for the upgrade.

43
00:02:36.377 --> 00:02:38.775
Do not talk about fire, or blame.

44
00:02:39.225 --> 00:02:41.266
If they keep asking, it goes to Ben.

45
00:02:41.266 --> 00:02:44.862
So they get a straight answer,
from the person who has one.

46
00:02:46.212 --> 00:02:48.888
Now, three ways to fix an old job.

47
00:02:49.338 --> 00:02:53.720
Fix A. New board. Use it when the old
job already has one wire per injection,

48
00:02:53.720 --> 00:02:57.100
and just never had breakers.
It takes about half a day.

49
00:02:57.550 --> 00:03:00.803
Power off at the breaker.
Fit the new board in the box.

50
00:03:00.803 --> 00:03:06.554
Put each existing wire on its own 4 amp circuit,
and the controller on its own 4 amp circuit.

51
00:03:07.004 --> 00:03:10.695
Count the lights per group,
and split any group over 70.

52
00:03:10.695 --> 00:03:12.989
Then meter every circuit on PW.

53
00:03:13.439 --> 00:03:17.508
Fix B. New wire. Use it when one
wire feeds several injections.

54
00:03:17.508 --> 00:03:20.342
There is no way around pulling new wire.

55
00:03:20.792 --> 00:03:24.246
Power off at the breaker.
Find every injection point,

56
00:03:24.246 --> 00:03:26.452
and pull new 16/2 to each one.

57
00:03:26.902 --> 00:03:30.250
Fit the new board, with
one 4 amp circuit per wire.

58
00:03:30.250 --> 00:03:34.965
Cut the positive at every injection.
Then meter every circuit on PW.

59
00:03:35.415 --> 00:03:39.941
Fix C. Small driver. Put a small
Class 2 driver right at the plug,

60
00:03:39.941 --> 00:03:43.003
instead of pulling wire all the way back.

61
00:03:43.453 --> 00:03:46.815
Power off at the breaker. Check
there is a plug at the roofline,

62
00:03:46.815 --> 00:03:49.009
and mount the driver in an enclosure.

63
00:03:49.459 --> 00:03:52.275
Cut the positive. The
driver feeds forward,

64
00:03:52.275 --> 00:03:56.849
and the data still runs from the
box. Then meter that group on PW.

65
00:03:58.199 --> 00:03:59.517
Let's lock it in.

66
00:03:59.967 --> 00:04:03.080
On a service call, find out
which system it is first.

67
00:04:03.080 --> 00:04:06.150
Do not change a Mark 1
or a Mark 2 in the field.

68
00:04:06.600 --> 00:04:10.379
See anything on the stop
list? Stop, and call Ben.

69
00:04:10.829 --> 00:04:14.289
Never add lights to an old system,
never fit a bigger breaker,

70
00:04:14.289 --> 00:04:17.441
and never leave an old run
live without metering it.

71
00:04:17.891 --> 00:04:20.608
Tell the customer we moved
to a new power system,

72
00:04:20.608 --> 00:04:24.573
and they are on the list for the
upgrade. No talk of fire or blame.

73
00:04:25.023 --> 00:04:29.075
Three fixes. A new board,
new wire, or a small driver.

74
00:04:29.075 --> 00:04:32.251
Every one ends with a meter test on PW.

75
00:04:32.701 --> 00:04:35.761
That is the end of the
course. Take the Part 7 quiz,

76
00:04:35.761 --> 00:04:38.013
and thank you for wiring it right.
